# Golf Project — Development Context

## 1. Project Overview

A 16-bit-style, turn-based golf roguelike being developed in Godot 4 using GDScript.

**Long-term goal:** A standalone PC game, potentially released on Steam.

### Core Concept

Two golfers compete across an 18-hole match, sharing the same physical course.

The game combines:

- Pull-back-and-release golf mechanics (similar to Angry Birds).
- Match-play scoring: winning individual holes matters more than total strokes.
- Card-based abilities inspired by Magic: The Gathering.
- Roguelike progression and build synergies inspired by Balatro.
- Procedurally generated golf courses.
- Environmental manipulation that affects both players.

Players visit a clubhouse after every three holes to purchase abilities and improve their builds.

### Three Gameplay Pillars

**Golfer:** RPG-style character attributes such as power, accuracy and curve.

**Clubs:** Equipment with different properties, buffs, debuffs, durability and potential breakage.

**Course:** Procedural layouts, materials, hazards, weather and physics modifiers.

### Card System

Abilities have timing restrictions:

- PRE_SHOT — Before taking a shot.
- POST_SHOT — After a shot finishes.
- INSTANT — Can be activated during reaction windows, including an opponent's turn.

Card activation does not cost energy. Cards are balanced through purchase price, hand-size limitations, availability and timing restrictions.

Target categories: NONE, BALL, COURSE_AREA, GOLFER, GAME.

GAME abilities can manipulate match rules, scorecards, stroke counts and similar systems.

## 2. Technical Setup

- Engine: Godot 4
- Language: GDScript
- Renderer: Compatibility
- Base viewport: 640 × 360
- Development approach: Small, independently testable milestones
- Repository: https://github.com/MancusMagpie/golfproject

### Important Scripts

- `golf_ball.gd` — Ball movement, friction, boundaries and shot signals.
- `shot_controller.gd` — Pull-back aiming, launching and cancellation.
- `turn_manager.gd` — Turn ownership, shot phases, stroke counting and turn selection.
- `reaction_manager.gd` — Reaction pausing and countdowns.
- `golf_ability.gd` — Data model for abilities.
- `ability_manager.gd` — Player hands, ability restrictions and card consumption.
- `targeting_controller.gd` — Ability targeting.
- `golf_hole.gd` — Golf cup rendering and ball capture.
- `hud.gd` — Temporary debugging HUD.

The current implementation uses two golfers, each represented by a separate GolfBall instance.

## 3. Implemented Features

### Milestone 0.1 — Shot Sandbox
- Pull-back-and-release aiming.
- Variable shot power.
- Friction-based ball movement.
- Shot start and finish signals.

### Milestone 0.2 — Two-Player Turns
- Two golfers.
- Turn management.
- Right-click shot cancellation.
- Configurable post-shot countdown (currently 5 seconds).
- Ball boundaries.

### Milestone 0.3 — Reaction Windows
- Pause and resume ball movement.
- Configurable reaction countdown (currently 30 seconds).
- Instants can be activated during reaction windows.
- Reaction closes automatically when an instant is played.
- Players can reopen a reaction if they have another playable instant.
- Empty reactions are tracked for future penalties.

### Milestone 0.4 — Abilities
- Data-driven ability resource class.
- Configurable maximum hand size (currently 5).
- Cards consumed when successfully played.
- Placeholder abilities: Freeze Green, Repair Club, Sudden Gust.
- No energy system.

### Milestone 0.5 — Targeting
- Ball targeting.
- Course-area targeting.
- Targeting cancellation.
- Card consumption only after successful target selection.
- Shot controls disabled during ability targeting.

### Milestone 0.6 — Golf Hole (In Progress)
- Reusable GolfHole scene.
- Cup rendering.
- Ball capture at sufficiently low speed.
- Preventing completed golfers from shooting again.
- Stroke counting.
- Both golfers take tee shots before distance-based turn order begins.
- Configurable closest/farthest-to-cup turn priority.
- Hole winner or halve determined once both golfers finish.

## 4. Current Prototype Rules

- One turn equals one shot.
- Two golfers share the course.
- After both tee shots, the golfer closest to the cup currently plays next (configurable).
- Players cannot shoot again once their ball is sunk.
- A shot advances through PRE_SHOT, BALL_IN_MOTION and POST_SHOT.
- Post-shot phase automatically ends after 5 seconds.
- Reaction windows last a maximum of 30 seconds.
- Abilities are currently placeholders and do not affect gameplay physics.
- Test hands automatically refill every turn. This is temporary.

## 5. Outstanding Design Decisions

- Allowing certain instant cards to activate directly without opening a reaction pause.
- Penalties for repeatedly opening empty reaction windows.
- Whether turn order should ultimately prioritise closest or farthest golfer.
- Detailed golfer attributes and club properties.
- Ability persistence, purchasing and clubhouse economy.
- Procedural course generation.
- Match-play scoring modifiers and strategic concessions.
- Ball flight, loft, gravity and terrain effects.
- Multiplayer implementation.

